kosha location-varga

  -- where things are. the spatial grammar of everyday knowing.
  -- not coordinates — pointing. "north of", "inside", "near", "above".
  -- location is always relative to something else — a reference entity.
  -- "delhi is in india" — india is the reference. "the book is on the table"
  -- — the table is the reference. the reference is always named or implied.
  --
  -- location-varga in the formal domain: position in sthira-apeksha.
  -- the apeksha (reference frame) is just named explicitly in everyday language
  -- rather than assumed to be the global inertial frame.
  -- "north of delhi" = displacement from delhi in the north direction.
  -- the common-sense pointing and the physics formalisation are the same
  -- structure — the everyday version just names the reference body, not the axis.

  "common-sense-varga-vishesa"
  "physics-yukta"

  -- connections to formal domain
  "displacement-abheda"    -- location IS displacement from a reference
  "apeksha-yukta"          -- every location has an implicit reference entity
  "direction-yukta"        -- above/below/north/south are direction qualifiers
  "sthira-apeksha-yukta"   -- the reference is treated as fixed (sthira)

